WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.719
Film Courage: Let’s talk about the good,
bad and ugly of screenwriting meetings.

00:00:03.719 --> 00:00:09.900
So how does a writer personally contribute
to the success or failure of pitching meeting?

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:16.520
Or maybe it’s not really within their control,
but they feel they’ve contributed to it.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:31.660
Larry Wilson: Well you do contribute to it
and it’s really grossly unfair to having

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:37.480
been on the other side of the desk, having
been a studio executive.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.640
Everyone has pitch nightmare stories and they
are true.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:47.280
But it’s really unfair to development people
and executives to assume that everyone in

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.609
those meetings is there to basically make
your life miserable and say no to you.

00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:55.449
It’s not true.

00:00:55.449 --> 00:00:57.809
And how you contribute to it as a writer?

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:02.579
You come in with an idea that you’ve actually
thought through and that you have some passion

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:03.800
for.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:11.180
And that also, you need to be willing to listen.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:20.369
It’s a conversation and the control you
have of the conversation is you’re the one

00:01:20.369 --> 00:01:25.070
coming in with the idea, you’re the one
coming in saying “Hey I think this would

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:26.180
be a great movie.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:27.490
This would be a great television show.”

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:33.030
Whatever…and you should have thought it
through and you should have prepared well-enough

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:37.760
to when they say “Well why do you think
it’s such a great idea?” to be able to

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:39.490
answer the questions.

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:46.490
And you should know enough about your story
that you can do it justice in the room.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:53.950
But then having sat as an executive, I’ve
also been in those meetings where you say

00:01:53.950 --> 00:02:00.719
to someone “Yeah, I get it but…”

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:03.750
And they immediately “What but?

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:04.750
There is no but!

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.090
This is the story.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:08.450
You have nothing to say to me!”

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:12.220
And a defensiveness goes up and that’s a
huge mistake.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:14.260
Don’t go to the meeting.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.940
Just write the stupid script.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.040
It’s a conversation and it’s a dialogue.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:26.010
And don’t assume that you know more than
the person in the room who you are speaking

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:27.010
to.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:31.790
Have it as a conversation and be strong and
know when you need to dig in your heels.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:38.601
And know that if people are changing your
story in the room so much that you don’t

00:02:38.601 --> 00:02:45.050
even recognize it anymore (which could happen),
that you need to say no and say thank you

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:47.440
for the meeting and leave.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.560
But engage in the conversation.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.510
Having a little bit of effing humility, for
gosh sakes, you know?

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:58.170
You’ve been invited into the room to tell
someone a story.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:02.770
Don’t assume that they don’t have a thought
in their head.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:05.870
And there are so many stories (and again),
a lot of them are true.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:08.800
And I could tell you nightmare stories about
pitches.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.490
We could stay here all night and I could tell
you those stories.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:22.590
But I could also tell you great stories with
great development people who have given me

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:27.160
enough rope to hang myself, then said “Well
what if this happened or that happened?”

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:30.090
And they’ve given me a better idea and a
better take.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:36.440
It’s a dialogue and a conversation and it’s
part of the business and I hate to call it

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.340
a game but I will for a moment, it’s part
of the game.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:44.320
But if you just can’t stand the idea of
going in and telling a story and someone’s

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:49.700
going to try and change it or have an idea,
write the script.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:53.530
Don’t be a pitch monkey, be a screenwriter.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:59.070
Film Courage: So then how does a screenwriter
keep their side of the street clean?

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:03.070
Whatever happens on the other side of that
desk, whether the person’s kind with their

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:09.900
suggestions or not, how do they keep their
own integrity…and remain poised and all

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:10.900
of that?

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.520
Larry Wilson: By having integrity.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:21.540
And knowing that you are going in with something
that you believe in and that you’re excited

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:22.960
about.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:28.260
And so if they completely don’t get it or
say stuff that you think has nothing to do

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:32.560
with what you just pitched with them, you
know within yourself that the story you went

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.520
in to tell is the story you want to tell and
you really believe it can be a movie and you

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.470
can be really excited about it.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.020
Integrity is an inside job.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:49.860
And if you’re looking to have your integrity
brought to you by Hollywood, oh my gosh!

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.000
Film Courage: No?

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.130
Larry Wilson: No!

00:04:54.130 --> 00:05:00.910
It’s an inside job and if I go in to pitch
a story, I don’t do it unless I believe

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:02.870
in it.

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:04.190
And I may be wrong.

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:07.960
I may be wildly wrong.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:17.680
But I won’t leave going “You know what,
I thought I’d put that one over on them,

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.700
but they didn’t get it but oh well.”

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.130
I mean I’ll go “I told the best story
I could.”

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.010
And then you let it go.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.010
You just let it go.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:28.010
It’s a good meeting.

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:31.170
It’s a bad meeting but you did your best.

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:33.350
And that’s an inside job.

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:35.110
And it’s a learned experience, too.

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:41.440
And you will come out of meetings wondering
who you are and what you’re going to be

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:42.570
when you grow up.

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:47.650
I still have meetings like that but most of
the time I come away feeling good.

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:50.380
Film Courage: Yeah…that was my next question.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:55.450
Because it sounds like you just have to go
through it, have a few [meetings] where basically

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:58.180
you are in the fetal position when you go
home.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:02.900
And then it’s less so, you’re just on
the couch and then the next time you just

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:04.260
take a walk around the block.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:10.930
Larry Wilson: Yeah…and I don’t end up
in the fetal position much anymore because

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:13.120
I believe in what I do.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:17.710
It doesn’t mean anyone else has to believe
in it, but I believe in it.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:22.810
Film Courage: But it sounds like it’s a
learned thing and there is no magic answer?

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:24.550
Larry Wilson: No.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:28.610
No, there is no magic answer and it’s one
of those things that I’ve seen people get

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:36.000
crushed in these meetings, crushed where they
can’t come back again and that’s again

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:43.470
that thing about having an over-developed
sense of perfection, perfectionism to say

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:47.139
it more simply that will be your worst enemy.

00:06:47.139 --> 00:06:52.410
You had a bad meeting.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:59.000
There was this time when everything was supposed
to be based on Joseph Campbell, remember those

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:00.000
days?

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:13.370
When everything you had to go in and if you
were going to pitch something and Joseph Campbell

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:15.980
was going to end up in the sentence.

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:20.850
And I was going in with a producer who said
“Now remember to get the Joseph Campbell

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:22.700
moments in there!”

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:27.380
And I was pitching, I was about three minutes
into it and the guy across the desk said “Oh

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:28.460
jeez.

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:30.160
Can we get over the Joseph Campbell stuff.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:31.949
Can you just tell me the story?”

00:07:31.949 --> 00:07:34.340
And the meeting died a horrible death.

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:35.880
And I should never have done it.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:38.330
I should have just said “Here’s the story.

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:43.330
If you see Joseph Campbell in it, good for
you.”

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:48.730
But those are those things…don’t trick
yourself, just go ahead and tell the best

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:49.460
story that you can.

